<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tvrtko I of Bosnia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1338–<a href="/years/1391">1391</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Stephen Tvrtko I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tvrtko I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, of the Kotromanić dynasty, ruled Bosnia as ban from 1353 and in 1377 was crowned king at Mile, claiming the Serbian crown through descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>. He extended his rule over western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Zachlumia and much of the Dalmatian coast, making Bosnia briefly the strongest state in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. His kingdom weakened soon after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia.jpg" alt="Tvrtko I of Bosnia" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tvrtko I of Bosnia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Tvrtko_I_of_Bosnia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tvrtko was the elder son of Vladislav Kotromanić and Jelena Šubić, whose marriage was held in 1337. His uncle, the ban Stephen II, was succeeded by Tvrtko directly, although Vladislav was still alive; the reason is unclear. Tvrtko was about fifteen, so his father governed as regent, and after Vladislav's death in 1354 his mother Jelena replaced him. She travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to obtain the consent of Tvrtko's overlord, King Louis I, and held an assembly at Mile confirming the privileges of the nobles. In 1363 a conflict with Louis broke out, and the defence of the Srebrenik fortress marked his first victory over Hungary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Serbian Empire broke up into autonomous lordships after the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Uroš the Weak</a> in December 1355. In 1373 Tvrtko and Lazar Hrebljanović, both backed by Louis, defeated Nicholas Altomanović, and Tvrtko took the upper Podrinje, Gacko and part of Polimlje with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mileseva-monastery">Mileševa Monastery</a>. A genealogy published in his new Serbian lands stressed his Nemanjić ancestry through his paternal grandmother Elizabeth, daughter of King Dragutin, and he was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in the autumn of 1377, probably on 26 October.</p><p>After Louis died on 11 September 1382, the unpopularity of the queens Mary and Elizabeth gave Tvrtko his opening. He took Kotor and Drijeva, and between 1385 and 1390 won large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. In late August 1388 his army under Duke Vlatko Vuković defeated an Ottoman and Zetan force at Bileća. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> was fought on 15 June 1389, he sent troops to help Lazar; after Lazar's successors accepted Ottoman suzerainty, his Serbian claim was only nominal. In May 1390 the Dalmatian cities and islands surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tvrtko died on 10 March 1391, while negotiating peace with Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and is buried at Mile beside Stephen II. He left at least one son, Tvrtko II, but a minor was not considered fit to rule, and Dabiša, a relative exiled for his part in a rebellion of 1366, was elected king; under him the realm began to decay.</p><p>Tvrtko is considered one of the leading medieval rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, who left a country larger, stronger and more influential than the one he inherited, with a flourishing economy and new trade centres. The historian Vladimir Ćorović saw him less as an ambitious conqueror than an able statesman, who appeared to Serbians as a legitimate heir and to Croatians as the preferable ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tvrtko I of Bosnia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1377 Ban <b>Tvrtko I</b> had himself crowned king at Mile, claiming the Serbian crown as well, and the banate became the Kingdom of Bosnia.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a></li><li><blockquote>Its protectors changed often: Hungary, <b>Tvrtko</b> I of the Kingdom of Bosnia from 1385, and pressure from Zeta under the Balšići.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-kotor">Commune of Kotor</a></li><li><blockquote>Ban <b>Tvrtko I</b> of the Banate of Bosnia, having won lands in western Serbia, was crowned king of the Serbs and Bosnia in 1377 at Mile near Visoko, claiming descent from the Nemanjić dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li><li><blockquote>He was aggressive towards his neighbours, plotting against Prince Lazar and <b>Tvrtko</b> I of the Banate of Bosnia.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-nikola-altomanovic">Lordship of Nikola Altomanović</a></li><li><blockquote>With <b>Tvrtko</b> I he destroyed Nikola Altomanović in 1373, and by 1379 he was the leading Serbian ruler, with Kruševac as his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Srebrenik, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobovac">Bobovac</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.69,18.51&amp;year=1372" aria-label="Srebrenik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4992 584 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tvrtko I of Bosnia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5442 854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.69,18.51&amp;year=1372">What was here in 1372? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
